{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-338",
  "title": "Agent Taxonomy",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Agent Taxonomy"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Classifying the Role of a Tool in Producing an Outcome**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Overview"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This framework addresses the classification of a tool's role in enabling a specific outcome. The focus is on understanding the *instrumentality* of the tool—that is, how it functions as a means to an end. Tools in this context include not only physical objects but also software, processes, laws, or conceptual methods. The analysis centers on identifying the most appropriate terms that capture the tool-outcome relationship, prioritizing those that convey the enabling and functional role of the tool."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Key Analysis"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Primary Classifications"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Means**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is the most direct and general classification. A tool serves as the *means* by which a user achieves a goal. It emphasizes the role of the tool as the channel or method through which the outcome occurs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Example:* A search engine is the means by which information is retrieved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Instrument**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This term highlights the tool's functional role in the process. It often applies in technical, medical, or procedural contexts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Example:* A scalpel is the instrument used for performing surgery."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. **Mechanism**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When the focus is on *how* the outcome is achieved, the tool can be described as the *mechanism*. This term emphasizes the inner workings or system-level dynamics involved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Example:* A lever is the mechanism that allows force multiplication."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. **Enabler**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This classification captures the tool’s capacity to *make the outcome possible*—especially when the outcome would otherwise be difficult or impossible."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Example:* Reading glasses are an enabler for visual clarity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. **Facilitator**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Similar to *enabler*, this term implies that the tool *eases* or *streamlines* the process, often without being essential."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Example:* Project management software facilitates team collaboration."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Secondary Classifications (Context-Dependent)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-24",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Vehicle**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Metaphorical usage for tools that serve as carriers or conduits for broader changes or functions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Example:* A new law can be the vehicle for social reform."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-28",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Platform**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Especially relevant for software or infrastructure tools that support a range of other functions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Example:* An operating system is a platform enabling various applications."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Less Precise or Incomplete Classifications"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-33",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Cause**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "While the tool is part of the causal chain, causality is more accurately attributed to the *action involving* the tool, rather than the tool itself."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-36",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Prerequisite / Necessary Condition**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Tools may be required, but this framing omits the *active functional role* they play."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-40",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-39",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Source / Origin**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The tool is typically not the source of the outcome; it contributes through *use* rather than *generation*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-42",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Catalyst**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Sometimes applicable, particularly if the tool accelerates a process, but does not apply universally."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-45",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-46",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Open Questions"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-51",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-47",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In what contexts does one classification (e.g., *mechanism* vs. *enabler*) better capture the tool’s role than another?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-48",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "How should classifications shift when the tool is autonomous or semi-autonomous (e.g., AI systems)?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-49",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Should the user's agency always be assumed when classifying a tool’s role, or can the tool itself be treated as an active causal agent in some domains?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-50",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "What distinctions arise when tools are conceptual (e.g., legal frameworks) rather than physical?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-52",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-53",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Implementation Considerations"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-57",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-54",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Ontology Design**: When building taxonomies or classification systems (e.g., for knowledge graphs), these distinctions can guide more precise labeling of causal and functional relationships."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-55",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**System Documentation**: Technical writing may benefit from selecting terms that clearly denote whether a component is a mechanism, enabler, or facilitator."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-56",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Process Analysis**: Business and workflow modeling may need to differentiate tools that are *necessary* vs. those that are merely *supportive* or *optimizing*."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-58",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-59",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Related Concepts"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-63",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-60",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Affordance**: The range of actions a tool enables for a user, especially in design and human-computer interaction."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-61",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Agency**: The user's intentionality in deploying the tool—a key factor in distinguishing between *means* and *cause*."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-62",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Mediation**: In communication and social theory, tools often mediate interactions, a role closely related to being a *vehicle* or *medium*."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-64",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-65",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Conclusion**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-66",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The most appropriate classifications for a tool’s role in achieving an outcome are **Means**, **Instrument**, **Mechanism**, **Enabler**, and **Facilitator**. These terms capture various dimensions of a tool's function—from being essential and central to making a process easier or possible. Selection of the precise term depends on the context, the nature of the tool, and the degree of agency attributed to the user."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-67",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-68",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "thoughts?"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}